============================================================
Important Design Decisions
============================================================

Decision 0002

TMView shall never depend on the Windows GUI.

The GUI owns TMView.

TMView does not know TMainForm.

This cleanly separates

Presentation Layer

from

Application Layer.

============================================================
Navigator Responsibilities
============================================================

TNavigator owns the navigation state.

Current members

    FCurrentDirectory

    FCurrentImage

    FSortMode

Responsibilities

• Current directory

• Current image

• Navigation state

• Directory traversal

• Image traversal

Does NOT

• Display images

• Load images

• Handle mouse input

• Cache images

============================================================
Subsystem Hierarchy
============================================================

Rather than making TMView own everything directly,
helper classes belong to TNavigator.

TMView

    │

    ▼

TNavigator

    │

    ├── TDirectoryTree

    └── TDirectoryImages

This reduces coupling and gives each subsystem a
single responsibility.

============================================================
Naming Decision
============================================================

The originally proposed

    TImageList

was renamed to

    TDirectoryImages

Reason

Lazarus already provides a class called TImageList.

Avoiding the same name prevents confusion and keeps the
source code self-explanatory.

============================================================
Professional Design Principle
============================================================

When deciding where something belongs, ask

    "Who owns this information?"

instead of

    "Where can I store this variable?"

Examples

Current image

    belongs to

        TNavigator

Current zoom

    belongs to

        TRenderer

Configuration

    belongs to

        TConfig

============================================================
Major Architectural Breakthrough
============================================================

Observation

Hamana starts almost instantly when an image is
double-clicked from Total Commander.

The first image appears immediately.

Only afterwards does the program prepare navigation.

Conclusion

MView shall follow the same philosophy.

Old startup idea

Open

↓

Scan directories

↓

Build tree

↓

Show first image

Rejected.

New startup model

Open image

↓

Display immediately

↓

Background initialization

↓

Current directory scan

↓

Recursive directory tree

↓

Image preload

↓

Navigation becomes progressively available

============================================================
Startup Philosophy
============================================================

The first image always has priority.

Everything else happens afterwards.

This maximizes perceived performance.

============================================================
Future Startup Phases
============================================================

Phase 1

Immediate

• Load requested image.

• Display image.

Phase 2

Fast

• Scan current directory.

• Build TDirectoryImages.

Phase 3

Background

• Build recursive TDirectoryTree.

Phase 4

Background

• Start image preloading.

============================================================
Project Philosophy Reinforced
============================================================

Professional software minimizes startup latency.

Initialization should be progressive.

The user should never wait for data that is not yet
needed.

============================================================
Object-Oriented Concepts Learned
============================================================

Fields

A field is a variable owned by an object.

Object ownership

The object creating another object is normally
responsible for destroying it.

Hierarchy

TMView owns TNavigator.

TNavigator owns TDirectoryTree and TDirectoryImages.

